---
/**
 * 404.astro — Site-wide "page not found" fallback.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THIS IS
 *   The custom Not-Found page for areyoustillreading.dev. Astro treats a
 *   top-level `src/pages/404.astro` specially: at build time it emits
 *   `dist/404.html`, which Cloudflare Pages serves automatically for any
 *   request that matches no static asset and no Pages Function route. This is
 *   the single most-hit "error" surface on the site, so it is intentionally
 *   tiny and dependency-light.
 *
 * SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
 *   Render a friendly, correctly-chromed 404 page and point the visitor back
 *   to two known-good destinations (the blog index and the home page). It does
 *   NOTHING else — no data fetching, no auth, no gating, no side effects.
 *
 * HOW IT FITS THE ARCHITECTURE
 *   - This is a fully STATIC page. It is prerendered at build (`npm run deploy`,
 *     Wrangler direct upload) into `404.html`. There is no runtime code here and
 *     no Pages Function backing it.
 *   - Cloudflare Pages routing order matters: a real asset wins, then a Pages
 *     Function/redirect can win, and only if nothing matches does Pages fall
 *     back to serving `404.html` with an HTTP 404 status. So this page is the
 *     LAST responder, not a router — it never sees the attempted path and must
 *     not try to reason about it.
 *
 * DEPENDS ON
 *   - `../layouts/BaseLayout.astro` — supplies <html>/<head>/<body>, the shared
 *     site nav/footer, meta tags, and the OG/SEO scaffolding. `title` and
 *     `description` are forwarded to BaseLayout, which owns the <title> and
 *     <meta name="description">. Keep those props in sync with BaseLayout's
 *     prop contract (title: string, description?: string, image?: string); if
 *     BaseLayout's signature changes, this page must follow.
 *   - The optional `image` prop is deliberately NOT passed, so BaseHead (via
 *     BaseLayout) falls back to its default OG/social share image for this page.
 *   - BaseLayout also renders the shared header nav, which already links to Home
 *     and Blog. The two links in the body below are therefore SUPPLEMENTARY,
 *     in-content escape hatches — intentionally redundant with the nav so a lost
 *     visitor has an obvious next step without hunting for the header.
 *
 * DEPENDED ON BY
 *   - Nothing imports this file. Its only "consumer" is Cloudflare Pages' static
 *     not-found fallback (the emitted 404.html). Renaming/moving it would break
 *     that convention and the site would fall back to a generic Pages 404.
 *
 * SECURITY / GOTCHAS
 *   - No user input is read, echoed, or reflected here (no requested-path
 *     display), which deliberately avoids reflected-XSS / open-redirect risk on
 *     the error path. Keep it that way: do not interpolate `Astro.url`,
 *     referrers, or query params into the markup.
 *   - Both links are hard-coded, same-origin relative paths ("/blog", "/") —
 *     safe, cacheable, and independent of any backend (Supabase/PostgREST,
 *     RLS, Resend, Turnstile, Dodo Payments). None of those systems are touched
 *     by a 404, by design: the error page must render even if every backend is
 *     down.
 *   - Because this ships as prerendered HTML, keep it lightweight; avoid adding
 *     client scripts or HTML comments (they would bloat every miss).
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

{/* Delegate all page chrome (head, nav, footer, meta) to BaseLayout. `title`
    and `description` become the <title>/<meta> tags for this error page. */}
<BaseLayout title="Not found — areyoustillreading" description="Page not found.">
  <h1>404 — not found</h1>
  {/* Two escape hatches to KNOWN-GOOD, hard-coded same-origin routes. Never
      derive these from the requested URL or referrer — see security note. */}
  <p>That page doesn't exist. Try the <a href="/blog">blog</a>, or head back <a href="/">home</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
